<!-- THE STORY -->
<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h3>He had spent a lifetime learning what to tell a kid.</h3>
    <p>It lived in his voice. On his phone. In a room with people who already knew him.</p>
    <p>No app. No website. No name he owned.</p>

    <h3>We started with what he already did.</h3>
    <p>He talks into his phone. He texts it over. It comes back as a finished track.</p>
    <p>That was the whole pipeline. No studio, no schedule, nothing new for him to learn.</p>

    <h3>Then the pieces stacked up.</h3>
    <p>Two domains bought and pointed at a live site.</p>
    <p>An app in TestFlight, sitting in Apple&rsquo;s review queue, rebuilt once for child-safety rules, because the audience is children and that is not optional.</p>
    <p>A ten-track album, built two songs at a time.</p>
    <p>Trademark paperwork sent to an attorney, so the name on all of it belongs to him.</p>

    <h3>One decision saved over a thousand dollars.</h3>
    <p>The plan was flash drives. Boxes of them, handed out at events.</p>
    <p>We swapped it for a printed sheet with a QR code. Same reach. A fraction of the cost. His team agreed on the spot:</p>
    <blockquote><p>&ldquo;More accessible and easier quick access as opposed to someone taking a flash drive.&rdquo;</p></blockquote>

    <h3>The name stuck before the launch did.</h3>
    <p>Somewhere in month three his team stopped using his old nickname and started using the brand one. In emails. On calls. Without being asked.</p>
    <blockquote><p>&ldquo;Great idea. I call him that all the time.&rdquo;</p></blockquote>
    <p>That is the part you cannot buy.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHAT THIS PROVES -->
<section class="ink">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What this proves</h2>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">Age is not the blocker. A phone and a text thread are enough. Five months later he owns a trademark, an app, an album, and a sheet of paper that puts all three in a kid&rsquo;s hand.</p>
  </div>
</section>

